---
# AI-Investor — Documentation Governance Protocol (STAFF+)

documentation: This rule defines mandatory documentation behavior for all changes in the repository.
Documentation is treated as a **first-class engineering artifact**, equal in importance to code and tests.

alwaysApply: false
---

# CORE PRINCIPLE

> If it is not documented, it does not exist.

Every meaningful engineering decision, architectural change, or feature must be reflected in documentation.

---

# TRIGGER CONDITIONS

This rule must be applied when:

- Code is added or modified
- Architecture changes occur
- A sprint is executed or completed
- Tests are added or modified
- AI workflows are introduced
- APIs are changed or extended
- Dependencies or infrastructure are updated

---

# 1. DOCUMENTATION IMPACT CLASSIFICATION

Every change must be classified as:

- **NO IMPACT** → no documentation update required
- **LOW IMPACT** → minor doc update required
- **MEDIUM IMPACT** → one or more docs must be updated
- **HIGH IMPACT** → architecture/ADR/README updates required

If classification is unclear → default to MEDIUM IMPACT.

---

# 2. REQUIRED DOCUMENTATION SOURCES

The AI MUST evaluate updates across the following documents:

## Core Documentation
- `README.md`
- `docs/AI-INVESTOR-CONTEXT.md`
- `docs/AI-INVESTOR-ROADMAP.md`

## Architecture Layer
- `docs/architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md`
- `docs/architecture/*`

## Decision Records (ADRs)
- `docs/adr/*`

## Quality Engineering
- `docs/quality/QUALITY-STRATEGY.md`
- `docs/quality/TESTING-STANDARDS.md`
- `docs/quality/TEST-DATA-STRATEGY.md`

## AI Engineering
- `docs/ai/AI-ENGINEERING-PLAYBOOK.md`
- `docs/ai/AI-GOVERNANCE.md`

## DevOps
- `docs/devops/CI-CD.md`
- `docs/devops/DEPLOYMENT.md`

---

# 3. DOCUMENTATION UPDATE RULES

## 3.1 README.md

Update when:

- New major feature is added
- Architecture changes
- Tech stack changes
- Project scope evolves

Must remain:
- concise
- high-signal
- recruiter-friendly

---

## 3.2 ARCHITECTURE.md

Update when:

- New layer is introduced
- Service layer changes
- Provider abstraction changes
- Data flow changes
- System boundaries change

Must include:
- updated diagrams (logical or mermaid)
- updated flow descriptions
- updated layer responsibilities

---

## 3.3 ROADMAP.md

Update when:

- Sprint progresses or completes
- Features are added/removed
- Priorities change

Must reflect:
- current sprint status
- next sprint goals
- backlog alignment

---

## 3.4 ADRs (Architecture Decision Records)

Create ADR when:

- Any non-trivial architectural decision is made
- A design tradeoff is chosen
- A framework or pattern is introduced
- Data model changes occur
- Testing strategy changes occur
- AI system design changes occur

ADR must include:
- context
- decision
- alternatives considered
- consequences

No ADR = undocumented architectural decision (not allowed for HIGH impact changes)

---

## 3.5 QUALITY DOCUMENTATION

Update when:

- Test strategy changes
- New test layer introduced
- Coverage approach changes
- Flaky test strategy evolves

Must maintain:
- clarity
- reproducibility
- interview explainability

---

## 3.6 AI DOCUMENTATION

Update when:

- New AI workflow introduced
- AI agents added or modified
- Prompt strategy changes
- AI guardrails introduced

Must include:
- prompt patterns
- validation strategy
- failure modes
- safety constraints

---

## 3.7 DEVOPS DOCUMENTATION

Update when:

- CI/CD pipeline changes
- Deployment strategy changes
- Environment configuration changes
- Build/test automation changes

Must include:
- pipeline stages
- quality gates
- rollback strategy (if applicable)

---

# 4. DOCUMENTATION QUALITY STANDARDS

All documentation must satisfy:

## Clarity
- no ambiguity
- no vague statements
- explicit system behavior description

## Traceability
- every major feature maps to code + decision + tests

## Consistency
- terminology consistent across all docs
- architecture terminology aligned everywhere

## Interview Readiness
Documentation must support:

> “Explain this system in 60–90 seconds at Staff+ level”

If not explainable → documentation is incomplete

---

# 5. DOCUMENTATION COMPLETENESS CHECK

A change is NOT complete unless:

- Relevant documentation updated
- No orphan architectural decisions exist
- README reflects current system reality
- ROADMAP is synchronized
- ADRs created when required

---

# 6. ANTI-PATTERNS (STRICTLY FORBIDDEN)

- Outdated documentation left uncorrected
- Architecture changes without ADR
- Hidden system behavior not documented
- README drift from actual implementation
- Inconsistent terminology across docs
- Overly verbose documentation with no signal

---

# 7. AI ASSISTANT BEHAVIOR REQUIREMENTS

When applying this rule, AI must:

- Identify all impacted documents
- Explicitly list required updates
- Never assume documentation is “optional”
- Prefer over-documentation over under-documentation for HIGH impact changes
- Ensure alignment between code, tests, and documentation

---

# 8. DOCUMENTATION PRIORITY ORDER

When multiple docs must be updated:

1. ADRs (if architectural decision exists)
2. ARCHITECTURE.md
3. QUALITY / AI / DEVOPS docs (if applicable)
4. ROADMAP.md
5. README.md

---

# FINAL PRINCIPLE

> Code defines behavior. Documentation defines understanding. Both must always match.

A mismatch between code and documentation is treated as a system defect.

---

# END GOAL

This documentation system ensures:

- Production-grade clarity
- Staff-level architectural traceability
- Interview-ready storytelling
- AI-assisted consistency enforcement
- No undocumented system behavior